import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const clone = `git clone https://github.com/lapoule-dev/tuile.git
cd tuile
rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown   # once, for the guardrail below`;

const build = `cargo check --workspace                                     # quick build
cargo check --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p tuile-core   # the wasm guardrail
cargo test --workspace
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`;

const tests = `cargo test --workspace > /tmp/tests.log 2>&1
grep -E "(test result|FAILED|panicked)" /tmp/tests.log`;

const relief = `# Terminal 1: a synthetic relief, served as terrain and imagery
cargo run --release -p tuile-heightfield-serve        # http://127.0.0.1:8092

# Terminal 2: the viewer, on that relief — no catalog, no token
cargo run --release -p tuile-wgpu-viewer -- \\
  --terrain-url http://127.0.0.1:8092/terrain/layer.json \\
  --imagery-template 'http://127.0.0.1:8092/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.png' \\
  --imagery-scheme geographic --imagery-max-level 15 \\
  --lon 10.0 --lat 0.0 --altitude 3000 --pitch 35`;

const globe = `CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=... cargo run --release -p tuile-wgpu-viewer
CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=... cargo run --release -p tuile-wgpu-viewer -- \\
    --lon 6.86 --lat 45.83 --altitude 6000 --heading 120 --pitch 25`;

const compression = `cargo run --release -p tuile-wgpu-viewer -- --imagery-compression auto   # or bc7, astc; off is the default`;

const elsewhere = `scripts/try-viewer.sh web       # the page, http://localhost:8091/ (WebGPU, or WebGL2)
scripts/try-viewer.sh mac       # the macOS application
scripts/try-viewer.sh ios       # the iOS simulator
scripts/try-viewer.sh android   # an Android emulator, or the phone on the cable`;

const snapshot = `cargo run -p tuile-wgpu --example snapshot -- <tileset.json> <out.png> [size]`;

const doc = `cargo doc --workspace --no-deps`;

const site = `cd site
npm ci
npm run check        # astro check: types and templates
npm run docs:api     # cargo doc --no-deps for the published crates, staged under /docs/api
npm run build        # static build → dist/
npm run preview      # serve dist/ locally`;
---

<Base
  title="Getting started — Tuile"
  description="Build, test and run the Tuile workspace: the cargo commands, the WebAssembly guardrail, a globe with no token, the viewers on every platform, and how to build this site."
  kicker="Getting started"
  heading="Build it, check it, fly it."
  lede="Tuile is a plain Cargo workspace. Rust 1.97 or newer, edition 2021, no build system on top, no code generation step to remember. The crates are used from the Git repository."
>
  <h2>Get the code</h2>

  <pre><code>{clone}</code></pre>

  <h2>Build and check</h2>

  <pre><code>{build}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The second line is the one that matters most. <code>tuile-core</code> compiling to
    <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> is the cheapest available proof that no renderer, no
    async runtime and no HTTP framework has crept into the core. CI runs it, with every
    other crate that claims WebAssembly, on every change.
  </p>

  <p>
    The workspace denies <code>unsafe</code> outright and lints <code>unwrap</code> and
    <code>panic</code>, denied in CI, so a clean clippy run means something here. The
    GPU tests run headless; on Linux they need a Vulkan driver (a software rasteriser
    such as lavapipe is enough).
  </p>

  <h2>Reading test output</h2>

  <p>
    One local convention worth adopting, because it has already cost time: never pipe a
    test, build or lint run through <code>head</code> or <code>tail</code>.
    <code>head</code> closes the pipe as soon as it has its lines and kills the run partway
    through, so the summary line never arrives and a truncated run is indistinguishable from
    a hang. <code>tail</code> shows nothing at all until the process exits. Redirect the
    whole thing and search it afterwards.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{tests}</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    And a green suite is not proof. Write the test in the same change as the behaviour, then
    revert the behaviour and watch the test fail. Say plainly what a test does
    <em>not</em> cover, rather than letting a passing run imply coverage it does not have.
  </p>

  <h2>Fly a globe, no token needed</h2>

  <p>
    The viewer takes its terrain from any quantized-mesh <code>layer.json</code> and its
    imagery from any XYZ template. The workspace ships a server of its own that makes both
    from a synthetic relief, so the first run needs no account anywhere:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{relief}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Drag to move the globe, right-drag to tilt and turn, wheel to zoom. <code>N</code> turns
    the view north-up, <code>W</code> shows the wireframe, <code>F</code> freezes the
    traversal so the camera moves and the selection does not. <code>--help</code> lists every
    flag and key.
  </p>

  <h2>The whole planet</h2>

  <p>
    The <code>tuile-wgpu-viewer</code> binary uses the public connectors for terrain and
    imagery, with an access token you supply at run time and never commit:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{globe}</code></pre>

  <p>
    It opens straight down on France from 2,000 km; the second line opens somewhere else,
    in degrees and metres. <code>TUILE_RECORD=path.jsonl</code> writes the camera path and
    <code>TUILE_REPLAY=path.jsonl</code> flies it again exactly. Imagery can be held
    block-compressed on the GPU — your choice, off unless you ask:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{compression}</code></pre>

  <h2>The same viewer elsewhere</h2>

  <p>
    One application, four shells. From the repository root, one command each — they read
    the token from a <code>.env</code> file, and the page and the Android build never see
    it: they ask a local proxy that holds it.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{elsewhere}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The page is the ES module in <code>packages/tuile-viewer</code>, which
    <code>examples/web-viewer</code> imports. A program with its own tile service reuses
    the whole application — window, controls, scripting, packaging — by implementing one
    trait; the guide is <code>docs/16-embedding-the-viewer.md</code> in the repository.
  </p>

  <h2>A 3D Tiles tileset</h2>

  <p>
    Tilesets are parsed, traversed and decoded by the core and drawn today by an offscreen
    snapshot, which runs the geometry server until the tiles settle and writes one frame:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{snapshot}</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>Plainly:</strong> the interactive viewers draw the terrain-and-imagery globe;
    opening a <code>tileset.json</code> in a window is not wired up yet, and no sample
    tileset is committed — bring your own.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating the API documentation</h2>

  <pre><code>{doc}</code></pre>

  <p>
    That writes rustdoc for every crate in the workspace into <code>target/doc</code>. The
    <a href="/docs">API reference</a> page explains which crates this site publishes and
    how it serves them.
  </p>

  <h2>Building this site</h2>

  <p>
    The site is an Astro application built statically: the build produces a folder of
    files, the generated rustdoc among them, and no server of its own. It needs Node 22.23
    or newer.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{site}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>npm run docs:api</code> runs a rustdoc build of the published crates and stages
    the output where the site can serve it; it is not part of <code>npm run build</code>,
    because a documentation build takes minutes and a copy edit should not pay for it.
    <code>npm run build:all</code> runs both in order. Nothing generated is committed.
  </p>

